## Fallback Behaviour

What CDB gets when LD can't give a normal answer. For implementation details, see [UI Integration doc — link].

> ⚠️ Items marked **TBD** need confirming against the current implementation before this page is published.

| Scenario | LD / SDK behaviour | CDB behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| **LD unreachable at app start** (network, LD outage, blocked domain) | SDK can't initialize; flag lookups return the **default value defined in code** | **TBD:** Are the code defaults "safe" (feature off)? Is there an init timeout, and what is it? |
| **LD drops mid-session** | SDK keeps the last values it received; updates stop until the connection returns | Session continues on last-known values |
| **Flag deleted or key mistyped** | Flag isn't in the payload; lookup returns the **code default** | Same as above. Deleting a flag that's still referenced in code silently reverts to the code default |
| **Flag targeting turned OFF** | Serves the flag's **off variation** to everyone in that LD environment | Off variation applies to all QA envs (QA) or both slots (PROD) |
| **Context missing an attribute** (e.g. no `releaseVersion`) | Rules that use that attribute don't match; user falls through to the **default rule** | Gets the default rule value |
| **Cached flags from a previous session** | **TBD:** Is SDK local caching / bootstrap enabled? | **TBD** |

### Kill switch

`CDB_KILL_SWITCH`: **TBD**. Document what it disables (whole app? specific journeys? a maintenance page?), who can flip it, and the expected time to take effect. Remember that in PROD it affects **both** Blue and Green unless it's targeted by release version.

### Rules of thumb

- **Code defaults must be the safe state.** For a new feature, the code default should be off, so an LD outage never exposes unfinished work.
- **Don't delete a flag until its code reference is gone.** Archive or retire it only after the code no longer reads it.
- **"Off" isn't the same as "false."** Turning targeting off serves the *off variation*, which is whatever's configured, not necessarily `false`.